var o=[{title:`Reading the scale`,text:`RIR 0 means you could not have done another full rep with the agreed technique and range. RIR 1 means perhaps one more, RIR 2 about two, and so on. In the common RIR-based RPE system for lifting, 0 RIR is roughly set RPE 10, 2 RIR is RPE 8, and 3 RIR is RPE 7.`},{title:`Why it helps`,text:`The same 20 kg dumbbell can feel easy one day and hard the next. Estimating RIR lets you adjust for how ready you are that day while still aiming for a similar level of effort. It is especially handy when your weights go up in fixed jumps or when a bodyweight exercise can’t be adjusted in small steps.`},{title:`Example`,text:`Your plan calls for three sets of 8–12 rows at 2 RIR. At 12 kg you do 12 controlled reps and think you could do six more, so the load is probably too light. At 18 kg your form breaks at rep seven, so it is too heavy for this target. At 15 kg you do ten clean reps with about two more possible, which fits.`},{title:`Limits of accuracy`,text:`Beginners often find it hard to predict how many reps are left, especially on unfamiliar exercises or in sets far from failure. Exercise complexity, set length and fatigue also affect the estimate. You can get more accurate over time by checking your guesses against an occasional hard set done safely under supervision.`},{title:`Programming`,text:`Leaving two or three reps in reserve works well as a default for general resistance training. Some goals, exercises and experience levels call for more or fewer. Chasing 0 RIR on every hard movement adds fatigue, and you don’t need it to make gains.`},{title:`Using TrainUp`,text:`Use RIR to pick your load and keep your technique honest during sets. The effort rating you give after the workout covers the whole session, so it doesn’t tell you the RIR of each set.`}];var a=[{label:`Zourdos et al. RIR-based scale`,url:`https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26049792/`},{label:`2026 ACSM position stand`,url:`https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41843416/`}];var s={sections:o,sources:a};export{s as default,o as sections,a as sources};